  "body": {
    "id": "thm:impossibility-bounded-information",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Fix \\(C<\\infty\\).  For any possibly abstaining rule whose probability of an incorrect classification is uniformly at most \\(\\alpha\\) on \\(\\Theta_K\\), there exists \\(\\kappa=\\kappa(C,\\alpha,m,M,d,E)>0\\) such that along every sequence \\(\\theta_n\\in\\Theta_K\\) with \\(\\delta_n(\\theta_n)\\le C\\), the probability of correctly certifying \\(h(\\theta_n)\\) is at most \\(1-\\kappa\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Cbound",
      "alpha",
      "ThetaK",
      "m",
      "M",
      "d",
      "E",
      "theta",
      "n",
      "deltan",
      "h"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:independent-environment-samples",
      "def:classification-information-distance",
      "thm:honest-family-confidence"
    ],
    "justification": "A matching impossibility result makes information distance a genuine certification boundary rather than only a sufficient margin.",
    "gap": "Weak-heteroskedasticity tests and repeated-eigenvalue analyses do not construct opposite labeled causal-target strata while preserving acyclicity, stationary confounding, measurement error, range inclusion, and positive noises.",
    "consumer": "The bound prevents overconfident effective-target declarations when covariance profiles are statistically indistinguishable.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(C<\\infty\\), and let \\(\\theta_n\\in\\Theta_K\\) satisfy \\(\\delta_n(\\theta_n)\\le C\\).  Finiteness of this infimum implies that \\(\\mathcal A_h(\\theta_n)\\ne\\varnothing\\).  By the defining property of an infimum, without assuming attainment, choose \\(\\zeta_n=(Z_{0n},\\ldots,Z_{En})\\in\\Theta_K\\) such that\n\\[\n h(\\zeta_n)\\ne h(\\theta_n),\n \\qquad\n \\lVert\\zeta_n-\\theta_n\\rVert_{I,n,\\theta_n}<C+1. \\tag{32}\n\\]\n\nPut \\(R=M/m>1\\), and choose \\(p>1\\) sufficiently close to one that\n\\(p-(p-1)R>0\\).  Consider one observation with covariance \\(\\Sigma\\) under the denominator law and covariance \\(Z\\) under the numerator law.  If \\(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d\\) are the eigenvalues of \\(\\Sigma^{-1/2}Z\\Sigma^{-1/2}\\), then the covariance bounds imply \\(R^{-1}\\le\\lambda_j\\le R\\).  Whitening the Gaussian densities and evaluating the resulting one-dimensional Gaussian integrals gives, for \\(L=dN(0,Z)/dN(0,\\Sigma)\\),\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_\\Sigma L^p\n =\\prod_{j=1}^d\n \\lambda_j^{-(p-1)/2}\\{p-(p-1)\\lambda_j\\}^{-1/2}. \\tag{33}\n\\]\nThe chosen restriction on \\(p\\) makes every denominator positive.  Define\n\\[\n g_p(\\lambda)=-\\frac{p-1}{2}\\log\\lambda\n -\\frac12\\log\\{p-(p-1)\\lambda\\}.\n\\]\nOne has \\(g_p(1)=g_p'(1)=0\\), and \\(g_p''\\) is bounded in absolute value by some finite \\(K_0=K_0(p,R)\\) on \\([R^{-1},R]\\).  Taylor's theorem thus gives\n\\[\n g_p(\\lambda)\\le\\frac{K_0}{2}(\\lambda-1)^2. \\tag{34}\n\\]\n\nMultiply (33) over the independent observations and environments.  For each environment,\n\\[\n \\sum_{j=1}^d(\\lambda_{ej}-1)^2\n =\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left[\n \\Sigma_{en}^{-1}(Z_{en}-\\Sigma_{en})\n \\Sigma_{en}^{-1}(Z_{en}-\\Sigma_{en})\\right].\n\\]\nEquations (32)--(34) and the factor \\(1/2\\) in the definition of the information norm yield\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_{\\theta_n}\\left(\\frac{dP_{\\zeta_n}}{dP_{\\theta_n}}\\right)^p\n \\le \\exp\\!\\left(K_0\\lVert\\zeta_n-\\theta_n\\rVert_{I,n,\\theta_n}^2\\right)\n \\le K_C:=\\exp\\{K_0(C+1)^2\\}<\\infty. \\tag{35}\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of \\(n\\), the sampling allocation, and the selected pair.\n\nLet \\(A_n\\) be the event that the possibly abstaining rule reports exactly \\(h(\\theta_n)\\).  Since this classification is incorrect under \\(\\zeta_n\\), uniform \\(\\alpha\\)-honesty gives \\(P_{\\zeta_n}(A_n)\\le\\alpha\\).  With \\(L_n=dP_{\\zeta_n}/dP_{\\theta_n}\\), H\\\"older's inequality and (35) give\n\\[\n 1-\\alpha\n \\le P_{\\zeta_n}(A_n^{\\mathsf c})\n =\\mathbb E_{\\theta_n}[L_n\\mathbf 1_{A_n^{\\mathsf c}}]\n \\le K_C^{1/p}P_{\\theta_n}(A_n^{\\mathsf c})^{(p-1)/p}. \\tag{36}\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n P_{\\theta_n}(A_n)\\le1-\\kappa,\n \\qquad\n \\kappa=(1-\\alpha)^{p/(p-1)}K_C^{-1/(p-1)}>0. \\tag{37}\n\\]\nHere \\(p\\), and hence \\(\\kappa\\), depends only on \\(C,\\alpha,m,M,d,E\\) (the displayed calculation in fact needs no extra dependence on \\(d,E\\)).  This is the claimed obstruction to correct certification throughout the bounded-information regime.",
